<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical} ogType="article" schema={[article]}><div class="shell page-wrap"><Breadcrumbs items={[{label:'DragonSword Guide',href:'/'},{label:'Teams'}]}/><article><header class="article-header"><p class="eyebrow">Team planning · Check one connection</p><h1>DragonSword Awakening Team Planning</h1><LastVerified date="October 19, 2026" label="Guide updated"/></header>
<QuickAnswer title="Check who supplies the condition and who can use it."><p>Choose two Heroes you can use. Read the receiving Hero’s Signal Skill, then look for a skill on the other Hero that supplies its required condition. Use the worksheet below to separate a possible match from a handoff you have actually observed. This is a team-planning aid, not a recommended roster or a strongest-team ranking.</p></QuickAnswer>
<div class="copy">
<h2>1. Read the connection in one direction</h2>
<p>Write the pair as <strong>A → B</strong>: A supplies the condition; B is the intended receiver. Copy the relevant wording from your installed game version, including any extra requirements. A shared theme or a role label is not evidence that the skills connect.</p>
<p>The official combat overview connects Active Skills, Status Ailments and Signal Skills. It does not assign every team a mandatory Starter, Connector and Finisher. Use the actual skill descriptions to assess your pair.</p>
<h2>2. Copy this handoff worksheet into your notes</h2>
<table>
<caption>One record per direction and encounter</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Field</th><th scope="col">What to record</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">Context</th><td>Game version, encounter and the pair A → B.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Receiver B</th><td>Signal Skill name and its exact required condition.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Supplier A</th><td>Skill name and the condition its description says it supplies.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Extra checks</th><td>Additional conditions written on either skill; mark anything unclear as unknown.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">In combat</th><td>Was the required condition present? Did B’s prompt appear? Could you activate the intended Signal? Record what happened.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Conclusion</th><td>Observed handoff / not demonstrated / insufficient information, with the reason.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<ul><li><strong>Observed handoff:</strong> you saw the required condition and activated B’s intended Signal. Keep that conclusion tied to this pair, direction and encounter; it does not rank the team’s strength.</li><li><strong>Not demonstrated:</strong> the attempt did not produce the intended handoff. Record the missing observation rather than declaring the pair impossible.</li><li><strong>Insufficient information:</strong> the skill text, extra requirement or result is unclear. Leave the connection unconfirmed.</li></ul>
<p>If the prompt is missing, use the <a href="/guides/combat-system/">Signal Skills troubleshooting guide</a> for the trigger checks, then return to this record with the result.</p>
<AdsterraNativeBanner />
<h2>3. Check the third Hero separately</h2>
<p>An observed A → B handoff does not prove B → A or B → C. If you want the third Hero to continue the sequence, make a separate record for that direction. If you want that Hero for another purpose, write down that purpose and check the relevant skill text; do not invent a connection just to fill a “Finisher” slot.</p>
<p>Your result is a list of observed and unresolved connections among the Heroes you own. For equipment changes or before-and-after performance comparisons, continue to <a href="/builds/">Build Planning</a>; this worksheet only checks the intended connections.</p>
</div><SourceList intro="The official source supports the combat overview. The worksheet and result labels are our editorial method, not first-hand validation of a Hero pairing. Use your current in-game skill text for the pair you record." sources={sources}/></article></div></BaseLayout>
